  <h1>SOC Analyst Course in Delhi: Skills, Tools and Career Path (2026 Guide)</h1>

  <p>Cybersecurity is an important part of protecting businesses, networks and digital information. Organizations need skilled professionals who can monitor security alerts, investigate suspicious activities and help respond to cyber threats.</p>

  <p>A Security Operations Center (SOC) Analyst works with security teams to identify potential threats and investigate security incidents. If you want to build a career in cybersecurity, learning SOC fundamentals can be a useful starting point.</p>

  <h2>What Is a SOC Analyst?</h2>
  <p>A SOC Analyst is a cybersecurity professional who monitors security events, reviews alerts, investigates suspicious activity and escalates potential incidents to the appropriate team.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Monitoring security alerts and system logs.</li>
    <li>Investigating suspicious login attempts and network activity.</li>
    <li>Identifying possible malware or phishing incidents.</li>
    <li>Using security monitoring tools to review events.</li>
    <li>Documenting findings and escalating serious incidents.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Essential Skills for a SOC Analyst</h2>

  <h3>1. Networking Fundamentals</h3>
  <p>Learn IP addressing, DNS, TCP/IP, ports, firewalls and basic network troubleshooting. These concepts help you understand suspicious network activity.</p>

  <h3>2. Linux and Windows Fundamentals</h3>
  <p>Understand operating system commands, user accounts, permissions, system logs and basic administration.</p>

  <h3>3. SIEM Tools</h3>
  <p>Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) tools help collect and analyze security logs and generate alerts. Learn to review alerts and identify suspicious patterns.</p>

  <h3>4. Incident Response</h3>
  <p>Learn how security teams validate alerts, document incidents, escalate threats and support response procedures.</p>

  <h3>5. Cybersecurity Fundamentals</h3>
  <p>Study malware, phishing, authentication, access control, vulnerabilities and common security threats.</p>

  <h2>Tools Used by SOC Analysts</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>SIEM:</strong> Microsoft Sentinel or Splunk.</li>
    <li><strong>Network analysis:</strong> Wireshark.</li>
    <li><strong>Network discovery:</strong> Nmap in an authorized lab.</li>
    <li><strong>Endpoint security:</strong> Endpoint detection and protection tools.</li>
    <li><strong>Operating systems:</strong> Windows and Linux command-line tools.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Tool availability and employer requirements vary. Focus on understanding the concepts as well as practising with tools.</p>

  <h2>How to Start a SOC Analyst Career</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Learn computer hardware and networking basics.</li>
    <li>Practise Windows and Linux administration.</li>
    <li>Study cybersecurity fundamentals.</li>
    <li>Learn how logs, alerts and SIEM tools work.</li>
    <li>Practise investigating sample security events in a legal training lab.</li>
    <li>Build practical projects and document what you learn.</li>
    <li>Prepare your resume and practise technical interview questions.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Career Opportunities After SOC Training</h2>
  <p>Depending on your skills, practical experience and employer requirements, related roles may include:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Junior SOC Analyst</li>
    <li>Security Operations Analyst</li>
    <li>Cybersecurity Analyst</li>
    <li>Security Monitoring Analyst</li>
    <li>Incident Response Support Analyst</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A course alone does not guarantee employment. Practical skills, communication, problem-solving and continued learning are also important.</p>

